{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9bd78744953518da","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E009\n3\ttitle: Extend TrustyAI LMEvalJob as the single evaluation execution path\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n5\tcomponent: TrustyAI\n6\tteam: TrustyAI\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tbranch: A\n10\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n11\tdependencies:\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E001\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-9002-E002\n14\tgate_failure_impact:\n15\t  action: remove\n16\t  fallback_approach: If the execution-engine investigation does not select extending\n17\t    LMEvalJob, this epic is dropped and the new-evaluation-service branch (repo/license/konflux\n18\t    onboarding, service build, Llama Stack provider wiring) is executed instead.\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 1\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: 0\n25\t  external_dependency: 0\n26\t  human_process_gates: 0\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: High\n32\tai_implementability_score: 6\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\t---\n35\t# Extend TrustyAI LMEvalJob as the single evaluation execution path\n36\t\n37\t## Description\n38\t\n39\tBranch A of the execution-engine decision: build on TrustyAI's LMEvalJob (the\n40\tplatform's existing lm-evaluation-harness wrapper, currently Tech Preview) as\n41\tthe single execution path for GenAI model evaluation. Users can launch an\n42\tevaluation of a model served on the platform — a KServe endpoint or a Llama\n43\tStack distribution — against a selected benchmark suite, including custom\n44\tevaluation tasks, with results emitted in the canonical result schema.\n45\t\n46\t## Scope\n47\t\n48\t- Extend the LMEvalJob capability so an evaluation can target a model served\n49\t  on a KServe/ModelMesh inference endpoint or on a Llama Stack distribution\n50\t  (LlamaStackDistribution), not just the job's current target surface.\n51\t- Support user-defined custom evaluation tasks (own datasets and metrics)\n52\t  running through the same job flow as standard lm-eval task names.\n53\t- Emit evaluation results in the canonical result schema (per-benchmark\n54\t  scores, suite name and version, evaluated model version linkage) and persist\n55\t  them to Model Registry linked to the evaluated model version.\n56\t- Record cost/duration metadata (tokens, wall-clock, GPU time) on evaluation\n57\t  runs for capacity planning.\n58\t- Job lifecycle and status reporting sufficient for the Dashboard's run list\n59\t  and status views (running, succeeded, failed) and for pipeline-step\n60\t  consumption.\n61\t- Address Tech Preview maturity gaps identified by the execution-engine\n62\t  decision for the capabilities this path now carries.\n63\t\n64\t## Acceptance Criteria\n65\t\n66\t- **Given** a model served on a KServe endpoint and a selected standard\n67\t  benchmark suite, **when** an evaluation is launched, **then** the job runs to\n68\t  completion against that endpoint and records per-benchmark scores, measured\n69\t  by a completed run with retrievable results.\n70\t- **Given** a model served via a Llama Stack distribution, **when** an\n71\t  evaluation is launched, **then** the same flow completes against the\n72\t  distribution's endpoint, measured by a completed run.\n73\t- **Given** a user-defined custom task (own dataset and metric), **when** it is\n74\t  submitted through the evaluation flow, **then** it runs and scores like a\n75\t  standard suite, measured by a custom-task run with persisted results.\n76\t- **Given** a completed evaluation, **when** results are persisted, **then**\n77\t  they follow the canonical result schema and are linked to the evaluated\n78\t  model's Model Registry version, measured by retrieval from Model Registry.\n79\t- Evaluation runs record tokens, wall-clock, and GPU time metadata.\n80\t- Run status is observable throughout the job lifecycle.\n81\t\n82\t## HLR Traceability\n83\t\n84\t- HLR-1 (P0): launch an evaluation of a served model against a selected\n85\t  benchmark suite — full coverage for branch A.\n86\t- HLR-4 (P1): custom evaluation tasks through the same flow — full coverage.\n87\t- HLR-8 (P2): cost/duration metadata recorded by evaluation runs — full\n88\t  coverage.\n89\t- HLR-3 (P0, writer side): this epic emits and persists the results that the\n90\t  Model Registry persistence capability stores.\n91\t\n92\t## Dependencies (plain terms)\n93\t\n94\tExists only if the execution-engine decision selects extending LMEvalJob.\n95\tDepends on that decision and on the canonical result schema prototype (the\n96\tjob code emits that schema). If the decision instead recommends a new\n97\tevaluation service, this epic is dropped in favor of the new-service branch.\n98\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bafeac65b5675e9e","agent_id":"a0d5c3e4fb04950c0","session_id":"b48ac94b-45e8-413d-be21-820bec1a5f5b","uuid":"72c87cb8-c8b7-49a8-acc2-816a496083a3","timestamp":"2026-10-21T20:04:25.734Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9bd78744953518da","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9002 decomposition"}
