{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-fba4efbd-1840-4671-9980-a9b89c766309","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9261a779c6fa4067","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9002-j2d1wnw7/artifacts/epic-reviews/RHAISTRAT-9002-decomp-review.md","content":"# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9002\n\n## Review Summary\n\nScore: 11/14 — fail\nRecommendation: revise\n\nThe decomposition is structurally sound on coverage, DAG coherence, boundaries,\nACs, and completeness: all nine HLRs are traced (P0 HLR-1/HLR-4/HLR-7/HLR-8 via\nthe two conditional branches of the engine decision), diagram edges and epic\nfrontmatter `dependencies` match exactly in both directions, the Branch B\nonboarding chain respects every mandated parallelism rule (license ∥ repo,\nlicense ∥ konflux, konflux ∥ implementation with the Rule 25 \"build pipeline\ngreen\" AC on B-E012 in place of an edge), all branch files set both `branch` and\n`gated_by`, no main-plan epic depends on a branch epic, and no epic body\nreferences siblings by draft ID. However, Criterion 4 contains one major issue:\ntwo epics list an Investigation (E002) in `dependencies` without `gated_by`\npointing to it, so the auto-fail rule applies regardless of the total score.\n\n## Criterion Details\n\n### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n\nAll nine HLRs are covered. P0: HLR-1 via BRANCH-A-E009 / BRANCH-B-E012, HLR-2\nvia E004, HLR-3 via E005 plus writer-side ACs on the execution epics. P1:\nHLR-4 via the execution epics, HLR-5 via E006, HLR-6 via E007, HLR-7 via\nBRANCH-A-E010 / BRANCH-B-E013. P2: HLR-8 bundled into the execution epics and\nthe E002 schema, HLR-9 via E008. The traceability matrix is present and\ncomplete; no orphaned HLRs.\n\nPriority inheritance is correct: E001–E005 are P0 (each gates or delivers P0\nwork), the Branch B onboarding chain is P0 by inheritance from B-E012 (HLR-1),\nE006/E007 are P1 and enable nothing downstream except the docs epic, and E008's\nP0 correctly applies the `docs-authoring` exception (strategy `priority:\nCritical` → P0) — not a priority inversion.\n\nPriority-collapse check passes: HLR-4 (custom tasks) and HLR-8 (cost metadata)\nare bundled into the P0 execution epics, but both share the API, job-runner\nsurface, and result schema with the P0 work — per the splitting rule they do\nnot qualify as distinct deferrable features, and the decisions are recorded\nwith rationale in the summary. Triage is null (full decomposition), so the\nmulti-epic output is expected.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n\nNo circular dependencies. Every diagram edge has a matching frontmatter\n`dependencies` entry and vice versa, in all three subgraphs (main plan, Branch\nA, Branch B); arrows follow the dependency → dependent convention. All\nmandated parallelism holds: license-validation (B-E010) has no edge to\nrepo-onboarding (B-E009) or konflux-onboarding (B-E011), and konflux-onboarding\n(B-E011) has no edge in either direction to any general implementation epic\n(B-E012, B-E013, or the main-plan/Branch A implementations) — the coupling is\ncorrectly carried by the \"build pipeline green\" AC on B-E012 per Rule 25, and\nthe summary explicitly documents the deliberate edge absence. Branch files all\nset `branch` and `gated_by=E001` (with E001 in `dependencies`). No main-plan\nepic lists a branch epic in its `dependencies`; E006/E007 correctly depend on\nand are gated by E001 itself, and E008's inability to DAG-block on branch\nexecution work is handled with an accuracy AC. No image-digest/endpoint-URL\ndependency is wired as an edge. Critical path (4 on the main plan; 4 within\nBranch B) is reasonable for the strategy size. The deliberate absence of an\nE004 → execution edge is correctly reasoned as a Rule 13 configuration\nreference.\n\nMinor notes (below the 3-minor threshold, no point deduction):\n- The DAG justification table cites \"Rules 3, 13\" for the E002 →\n  BRANCH-A-E009 / BRANCH-B-E012 edges, but Rule 13 governs\n  Implementation → Implementation edges; an edge from an Investigation falls\n  under Rule 3, which requires a true gate (see Criterion 4 — this citation\n  imprecision is the same root cause as the major issue there).\n- E006's launch-entry integration (and E007's suite selection) consumes the\n  suite catalog registered by E004 with no edge or AC linking them. Treating\n  it as a runtime configuration reference is defensible, but an explicit\n  \"suite catalog available\" AC would make the runtime contract verifiable.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\nEvery epic maps to a single component/team tuple: TrustyAI (E001, E003, E004,\nA-E009, B-E009–B-E012), Model Registry (E002, E005), Dashboard (E006),\nPipelines (E007), Documentation (E008), RHOAI GenAI (A-E010, B-E013). No epic\nbundles work across teams. No scope duplication across siblings: A-E009 vs\nB-E012 and A-E010 vs B-E013 are mutually exclusive branch alternatives, not\nduplicates, and the E005 / execution-epic split is a clean writer-vs-store\ndivision of HLR-3 (E005 builds the registry persistence surface; the execution\nepics emit through it) rather than both building the same structure.\nWork-product types are not mixed: documentation is a separate `docs-authoring`\nepic (E008), and E007's usage material for the component it builds is\nincidental documentation, not a separate content-authoring stream. Epic sizes\nare plausible for single sprints; the execution epics are the largest but are\none logical change in one component and splitting them would duplicate the\njob/API/schema surfaces.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (0/2)\n\nMajor issue: **Investigation dependency without a corresponding gate.**\nBRANCH-A-E009 and BRANCH-B-E012 each list E002 (Investigation: canonical\nresult schema prototype) in `dependencies`, but their `gated_by` points only\nto E001. Per Rule 3 and the decomposer's own generation rule (\"Every epic\nthat depends on an Investigation should have `gated_by` pointing to that\nInvestigation\"), an Investigation dependency implies by definition that the\noutcome changes the downstream epic's scope or approach and must be recorded\nas a gate. The decomposition is internally inconsistent about this: E005 —\nwhich is blocked by E002 in exactly the same way and consumes the same schema\n— is `gated_by: E002` with `action: rewrite` (\"if the prototype revises the\ncanonical format... rewrite the persistence implementation\"), yet the\nexecution epics that *emit* that schema record no E002 gate. A schema\nrevision rewrites their emission implementation identically. The DAG\njustification's citation of Rule 13 for these edges does not apply: Rule 13\ncovers Implementation → Implementation edges; edges from an Investigation are\nRule 3 edges requiring true gates. Automated consumers that detect gates via\n`gated_by` will miss the E002 gate on the two epics whose output format it\ndetermines.\n\nEverything else in this criterion is correct: all three Investigations are\ngenuine (E001 selects between bounded branch structures; E002 determines the\npersisted format; E003 determines E004's packaging approach) and each has\ndownstream dependents; all `gate_failure_impact` objects have both `action`\nand `fallback_approach` populated; every `gated_by` target appears in the\nepic's direct `dependencies`.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n\nSignal fields are type-correct throughout (Investigation epics carry\n`investigation_signals`, Implementation epics carry `ai_signals`; no epic sets\nboth), all 15 ai-signals files exist, and every file's rationale table matches\nthe frontmatter values. One arguable inconsistency: A-E009 and B-E012 score\n`open_questions: 0` with the rationale that the result schema \"arrives from\nthe blocking prototype\", while E005 — blocked by the same E002 — scores\n`open_questions: -1` because the canonical schema is an explicit strategy\nopen question. The schema is an open question that would change the emission\napproach of the execution epics just as it changes E005's persistence\napproach; the same situation is scored two different ways. The values are\ndefensible as \"input delivered by a blocking dependency\" but are at minimum\narguable against the -1 rubric condition (\"open questions that would change\nimplementation approach\"). All other signal values are consistent with the\nrubric and strategy content (e.g., E004/E005/E006/E007 correctly carry -1 for\ntheir respective unresolved gates; B-E009/B-E011 correctly carry -1\n`human_process_gates` for org/tenant approvals).\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable Given/When/Then or verifiable ACs derived from the\nstrategy. Rule-mandated ACs are present wherever applicable: E008\n(docs-authoring) has the technical-review-against-implementation AC (Rule 24);\nB-E012 has the \"build pipeline green\" AC coupling it to the parallel Konflux\nonboarding (Rule 25), and B-E011 additionally has its own green-pipeline AC.\nNo epic in this set replaces an existing behavior in the rollback sense\n(A-E010/B-E013 wire a provider where none exists; E006 is feature-flagged via\nOdhDashboardConfig), so no rollback AC is mandated. E007 explicitly enforces\nthe strategy's out-of-scope boundary (pipelines gate, humans promote) as an\nAC. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID — dependencies are\ndescribed in plain terms throughout.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nThe combined epic set accounts for the full strategy scope: all HLRs (via\nCriterion 1), all three strategy open questions mapped to Investigations\n(E001, E002, E003), the out-of-scope boundaries respected (no auto-promotion,\nno arena-style evaluation, no fine-tuning feedback), and the two bounded\noutcomes of the engine decision fully covered by Branch A and Branch B with\nno gap between them. Strategy-level context is carried forward rather than\ndropped: the Tech Preview maturity risk appears in E001's and A-E009's scope,\nthe disconnected-environment constraint drives E004's ACs, and the missing\nstrategy sections (teams, NFRs, Given/When/Then) are explicitly flagged in\nthe summary's Health Warnings with the inference made visible. Documentation\nsupport is required by HLR-9 and the docs-authoring epic E008 is present\n(Rule 11), blocked by all main-plan Implementation epics. Cross-epic\nconsistency holds: every multi-item upstream scope (schema fields, suite\ncatalog, execution surfaces) is consumed by the downstream epics that need\nit.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":62913,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bafeac65b5675e9e","agent_id":"a0d5c3e4fb04950c0","session_id":"b48ac94b-45e8-413d-be21-820bec1a5f5b","uuid":"62d829e1-fbe2-430c-950b-2744994038b0","timestamp":"2026-10-21T20:13:11.587Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9002 decomposition"}
