{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a392a33b5f765a11","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9002\n3\tscore: 11\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: major\n8\t  criterion: Type Correctness\n9\t  description: BRANCH-A-E009 and BRANCH-B-E012 list Investigation E002 (canonical\n10\t    result schema prototype) in dependencies but gated_by points only to E001. Per\n11\t    Rule 3, an Investigation dependency implies the outcome changes the downstream\n12\t    epic scope/approach and must be recorded as a gate; E005, blocked by the same\n13\t    E002 and consuming the same schema, is gated_by E002 with action=rewrite, so the\n14\t    execution epics that emit the schema are treated inconsistently. The DAG justification\n15\t    cites Rule 13 for these edges, but Rule 13 covers Implementation-to-Implementation\n16\t    edges. Record the E002 gate on both execution epics (or restructure the dependency\n17\t    so the schema contract is not an ungated Investigation edge).\n18\t- severity: minor\n19\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n20\t  description: A-E009 and B-E012 score open_questions=0 (schema arrives from the blocking\n21\t    prototype) while E005 scores open_questions=-1 for the same pending canonical\n22\t    schema from E002; the identical situation is scored two different ways against\n23\t    the -1 rubric condition (open questions that would change implementation approach).\n24\t    Align the three values.\n25\t- severity: minor\n26\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n27\t  description: The DAG justification table cites Rules 3 and 13 for the E002-to-branch-execution\n28\t    edges; Rule 13 governs Implementation-to-Implementation edges, so the citation\n29\t    is imprecise for an Investigation edge (related to the Type Correctness major\n30\t    issue).\n31\t- severity: minor\n32\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n33\t  description: E006 launch-entry integration and E007 suite selection consume the\n34\t    suite catalog registered by E004 with no edge or AC linking them; defensible as\n35\t    a Rule 13 runtime configuration reference, but an explicit suite-catalog-available\n36\t    AC would make the contract verifiable.\n37\terror: null\n38\t---\n39\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9002\n40\t\n41\t## Review Summary\n42\t\n43\tScore: 11/14 — fail\n44\tRecommendation: revise\n45\t\n46\tThe decomposition is structurally sound on coverage, DAG coherence, boundaries,\n47\tACs, and completeness: all nine HLRs are traced (P0 HLR-1/HLR-4/HLR-7/HLR-8 via\n48\tthe two conditional branches of the engine decision), diagram edges and epic\n49\tfrontmatter `dependencies` match exactly in both directions, the Branch B\n50\tonboarding chain respects every mandated parallelism rule (license ∥ repo,\n51\tlicense ∥ konflux, konflux ∥ implementation with the Rule 25 \"build pipeline\n52\tgreen\" AC on B-E012 in place of an edge), all branch files set both `branch` and\n53\t`gated_by`, no main-plan epic depends on a branch epic, and no epic body\n54\treferences siblings by draft ID. However, Criterion 4 contains one major issue:\n55\ttwo epics list an Investigation (E002) in `dependencies` without `gated_by`\n56\tpointing to it, so the auto-fail rule applies regardless of the total score.\n57\t\n58\t## Criterion Details\n59\t\n60\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n61\t\n62\tAll nine HLRs are covered. P0: HLR-1 via BRANCH-A-E009 / BRANCH-B-E012, HLR-2\n63\tvia E004, HLR-3 via E005 plus writer-side ACs on the execution epics. P1:\n64\tHLR-4 via the execution epics, HLR-5 via E006, HLR-6 via E007, HLR-7 via\n65\tBRANCH-A-E010 / BRANCH-B-E013. P2: HLR-8 bundled into the execution epics and\n66\tthe E002 schema, HLR-9 via E008. The traceability matrix is present and\n67\tcomplete; no orphaned HLRs.\n68\t\n69\tPriority inheritance is correct: E001–E005 are P0 (each gates or delivers P0\n70\twork), the Branch B onboarding chain is P0 by inheritance from B-E012 (HLR-1),\n71\tE006/E007 are P1 and enable nothing downstream except the docs epic, and E008's\n72\tP0 correctly applies the `docs-authoring` exception (strategy `priority:\n73\tCritical` → P0) — not a priority inversion.\n74\t\n75\tPriority-collapse check passes: HLR-4 (custom tasks) and HLR-8 (cost metadata)\n76\tare bundled into the P0 execution epics, but both share the API, job-runner\n77\tsurface, and result schema with the P0 work — per the splitting rule they do\n78\tnot qualify as distinct deferrable features, and the decisions are recorded\n79\twith rationale in the summary. Triage is null (full decomposition), so the\n80\tmulti-epic output is expected.\n81\t\n82\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n83\t\n84\tNo circular dependencies. Every diagram edge has a matching frontmatter\n85\t`dependencies` entry and vice versa, in all three subgraphs (main plan, Branch\n86\tA, Branch B); arrows follow the dependency → dependent convention. All\n87\tmandated parallelism holds: license-validation (B-E010) has no edge to\n88\trepo-onboarding (B-E009) or konflux-onboarding (B-E011), and konflux-onboarding\n89\t(B-E011) has no edge in either direction to any general implementation epic\n90\t(B-E012, B-E013, or the main-plan/Branch A implementations) — the coupling is\n91\tcorrectly carried by the \"build pipeline green\" AC on B-E012 per Rule 25, and\n92\tthe summary explicitly documents the deliberate edge absence. Branch files all\n93\tset `branch` and `gated_by=E001` (with E001 in `dependencies`). No main-plan\n94\tepic lists a branch epic in its `dependencies`; E006/E007 correctly depend on\n95\tand are gated by E001 itself, and E008's inability to DAG-block on branch\n96\texecution work is handled with an accuracy AC. No image-digest/endpoint-URL\n97\tdependency is wired as an edge. Critical path (4 on the main plan; 4 within\n98\tBranch B) is reasonable for the strategy size. The deliberate absence of an\n99\tE004 → execution edge is correctly reasoned as a Rule 13 configuration\n100\treference.\n101\t\n102\tMinor notes (below the 3-minor threshold, no point deduction):\n103\t- The DAG justification table cites \"Rules 3, 13\" for the E002 →\n104\t  BRANCH-A-E009 / BRANCH-B-E012 edges, but Rule 13 governs\n105\t  Implementation → Implementation edges; an edge from an Investigation falls\n106\t  under Rule 3, which requires a true gate (see Criterion 4 — this citation\n107\t  imprecision is the same root cause as the major issue there).\n108\t- E006's launch-entry integration (and E007's suite selection) consumes the\n109\t  suite catalog registered by E004 with no edge or AC linking them. Treating\n110\t  it as a runtime configuration reference is defensible, but an explicit\n111\t  \"suite catalog available\" AC would make the runtime contract verifiable.\n112\t\n113\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n114\t\n115\tEvery epic maps to a single component/team tuple: TrustyAI (E001, E003, E004,\n116\tA-E009, B-E009–B-E012), Model Registry (E002, E005), Dashboard (E006),\n117\tPipelines (E007), Documentation (E008), RHOAI GenAI (A-E010, B-E013). No epic\n118\tbundles work across teams. No scope duplication across siblings: A-E009 vs\n119\tB-E012 and A-E010 vs B-E013 are mutually exclusive branch alternatives, not\n120\tduplicates, and the E005 / execution-epic split is a clean writer-vs-store\n121\tdivision of HLR-3 (E005 builds the registry persistence surface; the execution\n122\tepics emit through it) rather than both building the same structure.\n123\tWork-product types are not mixed: documentation is a separate `docs-authoring`\n124\tepic (E008), and E007's usage material for the component it builds is\n125\tincidental documentation, not a separate content-authoring stream. Epic sizes\n126\tare plausible for single sprints; the execution epics are the largest but are\n127\tone logical change in one component and splitting them would duplicate the\n128\tjob/API/schema surfaces.\n129\t\n130\t### 4. Type Correctness (0/2)\n131\t\n132\tMajor issue: **Investigation dependency without a corresponding gate.**\n133\tBRANCH-A-E009 and BRANCH-B-E012 each list E002 (Investigation: canonical\n134\tresult schema prototype) in `dependencies`, but their `gated_by` points only\n135\tto E001. Per Rule 3 and the decomposer's own generation rule (\"Every epic\n136\tthat depends on an Investigation should have `gated_by` pointing to that\n137\tInvestigation\"), an Investigation dependency implies by definition that the\n138\toutcome changes the downstream epic's scope or approach and must be recorded\n139\tas a gate. The decomposition is internally inconsistent about this: E005 —\n140\twhich is blocked by E002 in exactly the same way and consumes the same schema\n141\t— is `gated_by: E002` with `action: rewrite` (\"if the prototype revises the\n142\tcanonical format... rewrite the persistence implementation\"), yet the\n143\texecution epics that *emit* that schema record no E002 gate. A schema\n144\trevision rewrites their emission implementation identically. The DAG\n145\tjustification's citation of Rule 13 for these edges does not apply: Rule 13\n146\tcovers Implementation → Implementation edges; edges from an Investigation are\n147\tRule 3 edges requiring true gates. Automated consumers that detect gates via\n148\t`gated_by` will miss the E002 gate on the two epics whose output format it\n149\tdetermines.\n150\t\n151\tEverything else in this criterion is correct: all three Investigations are\n152\tgenuine (E001 selects between bounded branch structures; E002 determines the\n153\tpersisted format; E003 determines E004's packaging approach) and each has\n154\tdownstream dependents; all `gate_failure_impact` objects have both `action`\n155\tand `fallback_approach` populated; every `gated_by` target appears in the\n156\tepic's direct `dependencies`.\n157\t\n158\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n159\t\n160\tSignal fields are type-correct throughout (Investigation epics carry\n161\t`investigation_signals`, Implementation epics carry `ai_signals`; no epic sets\n162\tboth), all 15 ai-signals files exist, and every file's rationale table matches\n163\tthe frontmatter values. One arguable inconsistency: A-E009 and B-E012 score\n164\t`open_questions: 0` with the rationale that the result schema \"arrives from\n165\tthe blocking prototype\", while E005 — blocked by the same E002 — scores\n166\t`open_questions: -1` because the canonical schema is an explicit strategy\n167\topen question. The schema is an open question that would change the emission\n168\tapproach of the execution epics just as it changes E005's persistence\n169\tapproach; the same situation is scored two different ways. The values are\n170\tdefensible as \"input delivered by a blocking dependency\" but are at minimum\n171\targuable against the -1 rubric condition (\"open questions that would change\n172\timplementation approach\"). All other signal values are consistent with the\n173\trubric and strategy content (e.g., E004/E005/E006/E007 correctly carry -1 for\n174\ttheir respective unresolved gates; B-E009/B-E011 correctly carry -1\n175\t`human_process_gates` for org/tenant approvals).\n176\t\n177\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n178\t\n179\tEvery epic has testable Given/When/Then or verifiable ACs derived from the\n180\tstrategy. Rule-mandated ACs are present wherever applicable: E008\n181\t(docs-authoring) has the technical-review-against-implementation AC (Rule 24);\n182\tB-E012 has the \"build pipeline green\" AC coupling it to the parallel Konflux\n183\tonboarding (Rule 25), and B-E011 additionally has its own green-pipeline AC.\n184\tNo epic in this set replaces an existing behavior in the rollback sense\n185\t(A-E010/B-E013 wire a provider where none exists; E006 is feature-flagged via\n186\tOdhDashboardConfig), so no rollback AC is mandated. E007 explicitly enforces\n187\tthe strategy's out-of-scope boundary (pipelines gate, humans promote) as an\n188\tAC. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID — dependencies are\n189\tdescribed in plain terms throughout.\n190\t\n191\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n192\t\n193\tThe combined epic set accounts for the full strategy scope: all HLRs (via\n194\tCriterion 1), all three strategy open questions mapped to Investigations\n195\t(E001, E002, E003), the out-of-scope boundaries respected (no auto-promotion,\n196\tno arena-style evaluation, no fine-tuning feedback), and the two bounded\n197\toutcomes of the engine decision fully covered by Branch A and Branch B with\n198\tno gap between them. Strategy-level context is carried forward rather than\n199\tdropped: the Tech Preview maturity risk appears in E001's and A-E009's scope,\n200\tthe disconnected-environment constraint drives E004's ACs, and the missing\n201\tstrategy sections (teams, NFRs, Given/When/Then) are explicitly flagged in\n202\tthe summary's Health Warnings with the inference made visible. Documentation\n203\tsupport is required by HLR-9 and the docs-authoring epic E008 is present\n204\t(Rule 11), blocked by all main-plan Implementation epics. Cross-epic\n205\tconsistency holds: every multi-item upstream scope (schema fields, suite\n206\tcatalog, execution surfaces) is consumed by the downstream epics that need\n207\tit.\n208\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b5c9c8c774e19669","agent_id":"aa44b0c53f6d9eafc","session_id":"b48ac94b-45e8-413d-be21-820bec1a5f5b","uuid":"47c1cd71-324b-4845-93ba-a8c8702afded","timestamp":"2026-10-21T20:13:41.253Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a392a33b5f765a11","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9002 decomposition"}
