{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a66d0c7c98e92411","type":"tool_result","content":"decompose-agent.md\nfetch-agent.md\nreview-agent.md\nrevise-agent.md\n/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9002-j2d1wnw7/skills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:319:Add optional fields only when non-null: `implementation_type=<value>`, `branch=<value>`, `gated_by=<epic_id>`, `gate_failure_impact.action=<value> gate_failure_impact.fallback_approach=\"<text>\"`. Every epic that depends on an Investigation should have `gated_by` pointing to that Investigation (see Rule 3).\n/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9002-j2d1wnw7/skills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:353:1. **Every branch file MUST set `branch` and `gated_by` in frontmatter** (in addition to the normal epic fields), plus `gate_failure_impact`:\n/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9002-j2d1wnw7/skills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:355:   - `gated_by={ID}-E001` — the `epic_id` of the **main-plan Investigation** whose outcome selects this branch (must be a real main-plan epic). It **MUST also appear in this branch epic's `dependencies`** — `gated_by` is always a member of `dependencies` (the branch cannot start until the Investigation resolves).\n/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9002-j2d1wnw7/skills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:363:       branch=A gated_by=\"{ID}-E001\" dependencies=\"{ID}-E001\" \\\n/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9002-j2d1wnw7/skills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:369:2. **A main-plan epic MUST NOT list a branch epic in its `dependencies`.** Branch epics are not main-plan nodes, so a dependency on one (e.g. main-plan `E004` depending on `BRANCH-A-E003`) resolves to a nonexistent epic. If a downstream unit of work depends on the *outcome* of the Investigation, depend on / `gated_by` the **Investigation epic** ({ID}-E001), not on a branch outcome. If it depends on a *specific* branch's work, it belongs **inside that branch** (as another `-BRANCH-<letter>-` epic), not in the main plan. A branch epic's own `dependencies` MUST include the gating Investigation (its `gated_by`) and may also reference sibling epics within the same branch.\n/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9002-j2d1wnw7/skills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md:59:Check: Trace the dependency graph. Verify each edge against the DAG construction rules (boundary rules 1-3, investigation edges 4-5, implementation type ordering 6-12, implementation edges 13-16, external dependency edges 17-19, generation rules 20-23, AC rules 24-26). Check that parallel-eligible work (different repos, no shared artifacts) is not unnecessarily serialized. **Implementation-type parallelism (rules 7, 8, 10) — enumerate and check every pair:** epics whose `implementation_type` pairing is mandated parallel must NOT have a blocking edge between them in either direction. Check **every** `konflux-onboarding` epic against **every** general-implementation epic (a decomposition often has more than one general epic — e.g. a component controller AND an OLM/CSV packaging epic; check all of them, not just the most obvious one). Specifically, a `konflux-onboarding → general implementation` edge (rule 10), `license-validation → repo-onboarding` (rule 7), or `license-validation → konflux-onboarding` (rule 8) is a **critical** issue — score this criterion 0. The only permitted coupling between a `konflux-onboarding` epic and its component's general implementation is the Rule 25 \"build pipeline green\" acceptance criterion, never a `dependencies`/DAG edge. Do not accept a rationale that the code \"needs the pipeline first.\" **In particular, an edge justified only by an image digest / SHA / `RELATED_IMAGE` / endpoint URL is a configuration reference (Rule 13), which must be an AC gate, not a `dependencies` edge — flag it as critical, do not accept it as an \"artifact dependency.\"** **Conditional branch files (`{ID}-BRANCH-<letter>-E*.md`), when present:** each MUST set both `branch` (the outcome letter, matching the filename) and `gated_by` (the main-plan Investigation `epic_id` that selects it) — a branch file missing either is a **critical** issue because the pipeline silently drops it. And no main-plan epic may list a branch epic in its `dependencies`: a main-plan epic depending on a `-BRANCH-` node (e.g. main-plan `E004` → `BRANCH-A-E003`) references an epic that is never created as an issue, a **critical** issue — work that hinges on the outcome must depend on the Investigation epic itself, and work that hinges on a specific branch belongs inside that branch. Note: Rule 11 edges (all implementations → `docs-authoring`) are valid DAG edges but do not trigger priority inheritance — do not flag them as unjustified serialization or priority inheritance violations. Verify critical path length against strategy size heuristics (S: 1-2, M standard: 3-4, M with new component: 4-5, L: 5-7). **Cross-check consistency**: The DAG diagram convention is `graph TD` with arrows from dependency to dependent (`E001 --> E003` means \"E001 must complete before E003 can start\"). Verify that every edge in the decomposition summary DAG diagram has a matching `dependencies` entry in the target epic's frontmatter, and vice versa. If arrows are drawn in the opposite direction (dependent → dependency), flag as a critical issue — the diagram is misleading even if frontmatter is correct. Any mismatch (edge in diagram but not in frontmatter, or frontmatter dependency referencing a nonexistent epic) is a critical issue — score 0. **Cross-check completeness**: also scan epic content (scope, ACs, descriptions) for data/artifact dependencies not captured in the DAG — e.g., an epic that consumes a schema, image, or API produced by another epic but has no edge to it. A missing edge discoverable from epic content is a critical issue even if the diagram and frontmatter are consistent with each other.\n/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9002-j2d1wnw7/skills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md:71:- **2**: Investigation epics genuinely resolve uncertainty that changes downstream structure. Implementation epics produce artifacts. No misclassifications. All `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are correct.\n/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9002-j2d1wnw7/skills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md:72:- **1**: Types are correct but gating metadata has issues — `gated_by` set without `gate_failure_impact`, or an Investigation dependency missing `gated_by` on a downstream epic.\n/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9002-j2d1wnw7/skills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md:75:Check: For each Investigation epic, verify it has downstream epics that depend on its outcome. For each Implementation, verify it produces a concrete artifact. For every epic with a non-null `gated_by` field, verify `gate_failure_impact` has both `action` and `fallback_approach` populated — if nothing changes on gate failure, this is a scheduling dependency (belongs in `dependencies` only), not a true gate (major issue). For every epic that lists an Investigation in `dependencies`, verify `gated_by` is set — an Investigation dependency without `gated_by` is a major issue because by definition the Investigation outcome changes the downstream epic's scope or existence. Verify that every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies` list — a `gated_by` referencing an epic only reachable transitively is a major issue because automated pipeline consumers use the `dependencies` list to detect gates.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b5c9c8c774e19669","agent_id":"aa44b