-----|----------|\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E009 | Extend TrustyAI LMEvalJob as the single execution path | Implementation | TrustyAI | P0 |\n38\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E010 | Llama Stack eval provider backed by the TrustyAI execution path | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P1 |\n39\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E009 | Midstream repo onboarding for the new evaluation service | Implementation (repo-onboarding) | TrustyAI | P0 |\n40\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E010 | License validation for the new evaluation service | Implementation (license-validation) | TrustyAI | P0 |\n41\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E011 | Konflux onboarding for the new evaluation service | Implementation (konflux-onboarding) | TrustyAI | P0 |\n42\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E012 | Build the new evaluation service | Implementation | TrustyAI | P0 |\n43\t| RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E013 | Llama Stack eval provider backed by the new evaluation service | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P1 |\n44\t\n45\t`epic_count` counts main-plan epics only (8); including branch epics the\n46\tdecomposition defines 15 epic files. Branch epics are excluded from the\n47\tmain-plan critical path.\n48\t\n49\t## Dependency DAG\n50\t\n51\tArrows point from dependency to dependent (\"must complete before\").\n52\t\n53\t```mermaid\n54\tgraph TD\n55\t    subgraph MainPlan\n56\t    E001[E001 Investigation: execution engine decision]\n57\t    E002[E002 Investigation: result schema prototype]\n58\t    E003[E003 Investigation: benchmark licensing]\n59\t    E004[E004 Standard benchmark suites packaging]\n60\t    E005[E005 Result persistence and linking]\n61\t    E006[E006 Dashboard evaluation UI]\n62\t    E007[E007 Pipelines evaluation step]\n63\t    E008[E008 Evaluation workflow documentation]\n64\t    E003 --> E004\n65\t    E002 --> E005\n66\t    E001 --> E006\n67\t    E005 --> E006\n68\t    E001 --> E007\n69\t    E005 --> E007\n70\t    E004 --> E008\n71\t    E005 --> E008\n72\t    E006 --> E008\n73\t    E007 --> E008\n74\t    end\n75\t    subgraph BranchA[Branch A: extend LMEvalJob]\n76\t    AE009[A-E009 LMEvalJob execution path extension]\n77\t    AE010[A-E010 Llama Stack provider adapter]\n78\t    E001 --> AE009\n79\t    E005 --> AE009\n80\t    AE009 --> AE010\n81\t    E001 --> AE010\n82\t    end\n83\t    subgraph BranchB[Branch B: new evaluation service]\n84\t    BE009[B-E009 repo onboarding]\n85\t    BE010[B-E010 license validation]\n86\t    BE011[B-E011 konflux onboarding]\n87\t    BE012[B-E012 evaluation service implementation]\n88\t    BE013[B-E013 Llama Stack provider wiring]\n89\t    E001 --> BE009\n90\t    E001 --> BE010\n91\t    E001 --> BE011\n92\t    E001 --> BE012\n93\t    E005 --> BE012\n94\t    BE009 --> BE011\n95\t    BE009 --> BE012\n96\t    BE010 --> BE012\n97\t    BE012 --> BE013\n98\t    E001 --> BE013\n99\t    end\n100\t```\n101\t\n102\tNote: in Branch B there is deliberately **no** edge from konflux onboarding\n103\t(B-E011) to the service implementation (B-E012) — Rule 10 keeps them parallel;\n104\tthe coupling is enforced by a \"build pipeline green\" acceptance criterion on\n105\tB-E012.\n106\t\n107\t## DAG Justification\n108\t\n109\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n110\t|------|------|-----------|\n111\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates Implementation) | The licensing assessment decides whether standard suites are bundled for disconnected environments or customer-fetched; this rewrites the packaging epic's approach. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n112\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates Implementation) | The result-schema prototype determines the canonical format persisted to Model Registry custom properties; committing before the prototype is validated against real MMLU output would rewrite the persistence epic. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n113\t| E001 → E006 | Rule 3 | The Dashboard reads evaluation run resources whose shape (extended LMEvalJob CRs vs. new service API/CRs) is selected by the execution-engine decision. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n114\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 13 (API → consumer) | The Dashboard's per-benchmark scores and side-by-side model version comparison are built against the persisted-result surface Model Registry exposes. |\n115\t| E001 → E007 | Rule 3 | The pipeline step's launch/wait mechanism for an evaluation depends on the execution surface selected by the engine decision. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n116\t| E005 → E007 | Rule 13 | Promotion gating reads persisted per-benchmark scores linked to model versions. |\n117\t| E004 → E008, E005 → E008, E006 → E008, E007 → E008 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes the delivered workflow and is blocked by all main-plan Implementation epics. Branch-selected execution work is covered by an accuracy AC rather than a DAG edge (branch epics cannot be main-plan dependencies). |\n118\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E009 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates Implementation) | Branch A exists only under engine outcome A. True gate: `remove`. |\n119\t| E005 → BRANCH-A-E009 | Rule 13 (API → consumer) | The job code emits and persists results through the canonical-schema persistence/linking surface. That epic is gated by the result-schema prototype (Rule 3, `rewrite`), so the schema contract reaches the execution epic through a gated edge rather than an ungated Investigation dependency (an edge directly from the schema prototype Investigation would require a second `gated_by` target, which the single-valued field cannot record alongside the branch-selection gate). |\n120\t| BRANCH-A-E009 → BRANCH-A-E010 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer) | The Llama Stack eval provider adapter is built against the TrustyAI execution capability delivered in branch A. |\n121\t| E001 → BRANCH-B-E009/E010/E011/E012/E013 | Rule 3 | All branch B epics exist only under engine outcome B; each sets `gated_by` E001 with `gate_failure_impact.action=remove`. |\n122\t| E005 → BRANCH-B-E012 | Rule 13 (API → consumer) | Same schema-contract conduit as branch A: the service emits and persists results through the persistence/linking surface, which is gated by the result-schema prototype (`rewrite`). |\n123\t| BRANCH-B-E009 → BRANCH-B-E011 | Rule 5 (repo-onboarding → konflux-onboarding) | The build pipeline needs the repo to exist. |\n124\t| BRANCH-B-E009 → BRANCH-B-E012 | Rule 6 (repo → implementation) | The service code needs its repo. Does not block other repos. |\n125\t| BRANCH-B-E010 → BRANCH-B-E012 | Rule 9 (license-validation → implementation) | If licenses fail, dependencies change and the approach is affected. |\n126\t| (none) BRANCH-B-E011 ↔ BRANCH-B-E012 | Rule 10 | Konflux onboarding and general implementation run in parallel; the \"build pipeline green\" AC on B-E012 enforces the coupling. No edge in either direction. |\n127\t| BRANCH-B-E012 → BRANCH-B-E013 | Rule 12 | The Llama Stack eval provider wiring builds against the new service's API. |\n128\t\n129\tEdges deliberately absent:\n130\t\n131\t- No `konflux-onboarding → general implementation` edge (Rule 10, both branches of reasoning apply to B-E011/B-E012).\n132\t- No edge from E004 (suite packaging) to the execution epics: the execution path\n133\t  runs any lm-eval task name; packaged suites are content/configuration\n134\t  referenced at run time, not a build-against artifact (Rule 13 exclusion).\n135\t- No edge from E004 to E006/E007: the Dashboard's launch-entry integration and\n136\t  the pipeline component's suite selection reference the registered suite\n137\t  catalog at run time (Rule 13 configuration reference). The runtime contract\n138\t  is made verifiable by an explicit suite-catalog acceptance criterion on each\n139\t  of those epics rather than by a DAG edge.\n140\t- No tiered-delivery split: there is no external dependency with uncertain\n141\t  timing. Benchmark licensing (E003) affects packaging approach only, and the\n142\t  fallback (customer-fetch) is an AC on E004, not a separate epic.\n143\t\n144\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n145\t\n146\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Coverage |\n147\t|-----|----------|---------|----------|\n148\t| HLR-1 (launch evaluation of served model against a suite) | P0 | BRANCH-A-E009 / BRANCH-B-E012 | Full — the execution capability is branch-selected by E001 |\n149\t| HLR-2 (standard suites out of the box) | P0 | E004 (gated by E003) | Full |\n150\t| HLR-3 (results persisted, linked to Model Registry version) | P0 | E005 + ACs on execution epics (BRANCH-A-E009 / BRANCH-B-E012 write results in the canonical schema) | Full |\n151\t| HLR-4 (custom evaluation tasks) | P1 | BRANCH-A-E009 / BRANCH-B-E012 (scope + ACs) | Full — custom task support is part of the execution path's API and job flow; splitting it would duplicate the same data structures and job surface |\n152\t| HLR-5 (Dashboard runs, scores, comparison) | P1 | E006 | Full |\n153\t| HLR-6 (evaluation as pipeline step) | P1 | E007 | Full |\n154\t| HLR-7 (Llama Stack eval provider backed by platform service) | P1 | BRANCH-A-E010 / BRANCH-B-E013 | Full — provider backing differs per engine outcome |\n155\t| HLR-8 (cost/duration metadata) | P2 | Bundled into BRANCH-A-E009 / BRANCH-B-E012 as scope/ACs; schema fields covered by E002 | Full — see priority-split decision below |\n156\t| HLR-9 (product documentation) | P2 | E008 (docs-authoring) | Full |\n157\t\n158\tNo orphaned HLRs. Priority-split decisions (Step 4):\n159\t\n160\t- **HLR-8 kept bundled** (not a separate epic): cost/duration metadata shares the\n161\t  result schema and job-runner surface with HLR-1/HLR-3 work; it cannot ship as\n162\t  an independent epic with user value and does not have a separate UI/API\n163\t  surface. Recorded as scope and acceptance criteria on the execution epics and\n164\t  as a schema requirement in the E002 prototype.\n165\t- **HLR-4 kept bundled** into the execution epics: custom tasks run \"through the\n166\t  same flow\" (strategy wording) — same API, same job runner, same result schema.\n167\t  Splitting would force two epics to build the same data structures.\n168\t- **HLR-9 split out** as `docs-authoring` (Rule 11): substantive end-to-end\n169\t  documentation including custom task authoring is a distinct work stream.\n170\t\n171\tPriority inheritance: E001–E005 are P0 (each gates or delivers P0 HLRs — an epic\n172\tblocking all P0 execution work, the P0 persistence path, or the P0 suite\n173\tpackaging is implicitly P0). Branch B onboarding chain (B-E009/B-E010/B-E011) is\n174\tP0 by inheritance — it is the prerequisite path for the P0 execution capability.\n175\tE006/E007 are P1 (HLR-5/HLR-6). E008 is P0 via the `docs-authoring` priority\n176\texception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0); per that exception it\n177\tdoes not push P0 upstream to the implementations it depends on.\n178\t\n179\t## Conditional Branches\n180\t\n181\tE001 (execution engine decision) has two bounded outcomes that change downstream\n182\tstructure, so the execution-path work is decomposed per outcome:\n183\t\n184\t- **Branch A — extend TrustyAI LMEvalJob (Tech Preview) as the single execution\n185\t  path.** One execution epic (BRANCH-A-E009: KServe/Llama Stack endpoints,\n186\t  custom tasks, cost metadata) plus a Llama Stack provider adapter epic\n187\t  (BRANCH-A-E010) built on it.\n188\t- **Branch B — build a new evaluation service** that wraps lm-evaluation-harness,\n189\t  exposes the platform evaluation API, and backs Llama Stack's eval provider\n190\t  API. The service is a new component (no architecture-context file), so Rule 20\n191\t  generates the onboarding chain: repo-onboarding (B-E009) ∥ license-validation\n192\t  (B-E010) → konflux-onboarding (B-E011, after repo) ∥ service implementation\n193\t  (B-E012, after repo + license validation), then Llama Stack provider wiring\n194\t  (B-E013).\n195\t\n196\tBranch epics set `branch`, `gated_by=E001` (also in `dependencies`), and\n197\t`gate_failure_impact.action=remove` — if the other outcome is selected, the\n198\tbranch's epics do not exist. Main-plan epics never depend on branch epics;\n199\tepics that depend on the engine outcome (E006, E007) depend on and are\n200\t`gated_by` E001 with `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`.\n201\t\n202\t## Component Mapping\n203\t\n204\tAll five strategy components (TrustyAI, Llama Stack, Dashboard, Data Science\n205\tPipelines, Model Registry) exist in `.context/rhai-components.txt` and have\n206\tarchitecture-context files — no non-obvious mappings. The documentation epic\n207\tuses the canonical `Documentation` component. Owner teams are taken from the\n208\tarchitecture-context owning teams (TrustyAI, RHOAI GenAI, RHOAI Dashboard, RHOAI\n209\tPipelines, Model Registry); the strategy itself has no Impacted Teams table\n210\t(see Health Warnings).\n211\t\n212\tAll five components are Konflux-onboarded, so no main-plan onboarding chain is\n213\tgenerated; the only onboarding work lives in Branch B for the hypothetical new\n214\tservice.\n215\t\n216\t## Health Warnings\n217\t\n218\t1. **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n219\t   Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no\n220\t   Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were inferred from architecture-context\n221\t   owning teams and acceptance criteria were derived from HLRs; a human should\n222\t   confirm team assignments against the current org.\n223\t2. **Tech Preview foundation for P0 work**: TrustyAI's LMEvalJob integration is\n224\t   Tech Preview while HLR-1/HLR-3 are P0. Building the P0 execution path on a\n225\t   Tech Preview capability is a delivery risk; E001's recommendation must\n226\t   weigh GA-readiness, and this risk is exactly why the branch B alternative is\n227\t   preserved.\n228\t3. **Docs priority vs. HLR priority**: E008 shows P0 (mechanical Critical→P0\n229\t   mapping of the docs-authoring exception) although HLR-9 is P2. This is\n230\t   per-spec, not a priority inversion in the feature work — flagged so the\n231\t   discrepancy is not read as an error.\n232\t4. **Branch coverage of HLR-1/HLR-4/HLR-7/HLR-8**: these HLRs are only covered\n233\t   by branch epics until E001 resolves. Intentional (the epics' existence and\n234\t   shape depend on the engine decision) but reviewers should verify branch files\n235\t   are attached to E001.\n236\t5. **Docs epic cannot DAG-block on branch execution work** (branch epics are not\n237\t   main-plan nodes); an accuracy AC (\"covers the evaluation execution workflow\n238\t   as implemented\") substitutes for the missing edge.\n239\t\n240\t## Tiered Delivery\n241\t\n242\tNot applicable. No external dependency with uncertain timing exists in this\n243\tstrategy: the open questions are internal technical investigations with\n244\tdecidable outcomes, and benchmark licensing (E003) has a viable fallback\n245\t(customer-fetch of non-redistributable suites) recorded as an acceptance\n246\tcriterion on E004 rather than a separate epic or a tiered split.\n247\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bee1af9f44323e62","agent_id":"ac6fccb22a2a1fcbe","session_id":"b48ac94b-45e8-413d-be21-820bec1a5f5b","uuid":"ddfa9cd6-4f5a-4a47-9c43-0753edc412bb","timestamp":"2026-10-21T20:20:26.204Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a80a1a070d15d505","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9002 decomposition"}
